WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.120
In the first film, signals flipped pre-made factors to launch genes in minutes.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.520
How does a cell build many distinct DNA switches from few genes, and stretch a brief

3
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.580
signal into hours?

4
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:21.673
Many nuclear factors belong to the basic leucine zipper, or bZIP, family, including

5
00:00:21.673 --> 00:00:27.713
CREB. A bZIP protein cannot grip DNA well alone; it works as a pair.

6
00:00:28.973 --> 00:00:31.453
Two helices zip along a stripe of leucines.

7
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:38.533
Below that zipper, positively charged tips, drawn to negatively charged DNA, part

8
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:40.493
like chopsticks to grip two half-sites.

9
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:47.193
Pairing multiplies choices, because swapping one partner changes which DNA site the

10
00:00:47.193 --> 00:00:55.193
pair prefers. With n proteins that all pair freely, the count is n × (n + 1) / 2.

11
00:00:56.313 --> 00:00:58.913
That counts same-partner pairs plus mixed pairs.

12
00:01:00.273 --> 00:01:06.733
Take 3 proteins: 3 × 4 = 12; 12 / 2 = 6 distinct pairs.

13
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.507
In real cells, not every pair is stable.

14
00:01:11.787 --> 00:01:15.487
Two bZIP families, Jun and Fos, build the factor AP-1.

15
00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:19.317
Jun pairs with Jun or with Fos.

16
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:25.147
Fos cannot pair stably with itself, because negatively charged residues beside its

17
00:01:25.147 --> 00:01:26.407
zipper repel each other.

18
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.100
Try the partner rule yourself.

19
00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.040
Suppose a cell makes 2 Jun proteins and 2 Fos proteins.

20
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.860
Pause and predict: with no Fos–Fos pairs allowed, how many distinct pairs can form?

21
00:01:46.867 --> 00:01:50.507
Seven pairs, because the rule removes only the Fos–Fos pairs.

22
00:01:51.947 --> 00:01:57.487
2 Jun proteins make 3 Jun–Jun pairs: 2 × 3 / 2 = 3.

23
00:01:58.527 --> 00:02:01.247
2 Fos × 2 Jun add 4 mixed pairs.

24
00:02:02.507 --> 00:02:08.967
3 + 4 = 7. Four free proteins would make 4 × 5 / 2 = 10.

25
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.030
Swapping partners also tunes DNA grip.

26
00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.510
A Fos–Jun pair holds DNA more tightly than Jun–Jun.

27
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:24.370
It also prefers a 7-letter DNA site, one letter shorter than CREB's 8-letter site.

28
00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.250
Back to the first film's gene, c-fos.

29
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.590
Pre-made factors switch on c-fos transcripts within minutes.

30
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.080
When ribosomes are running, those transcripts build new Fos protein.

31
00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.020
Fos enters the nucleus and zips with Jun to form AP-1.

32
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:51.540
On immune genes like interleukin-2, AP-1 sits beside NFAT from the first film.

33
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.160
Two pathways then meet at one DNA site.

34
00:02:56.183 --> 00:03:00.563
Paired factors widen gene choices and drive a slower second wave.

35
00:03:02.163 --> 00:03:05.863
Newly assembled AP-1 switches on later genes over hours.

36
00:03:07.163 --> 00:03:12.423
Because that wave waits for newly made Fos and other first-wave proteins, a brief

37
00:03:12.423 --> 00:03:14.443
signal becomes a lasting program.

38
00:03:15.923 --> 00:03:19.643
Staining Fos hours later marks neurons that were strongly excited.
